course: everyday
lesson: 415
title: Gift cards and birthday money: making it last
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Leo got three gift cards for his birthday and felt like a millionaire. By nine o'clock that evening he had spent all three — a skin, a glider, an emote of a dancing hot dog — and at breakfast the next morning he had nothing left to look forward to. «Money spent fast is fun fast and gone fast,» said his gran, buttering toast, as if she had been waiting years to say it. Mia did the opposite: she wanted a new game, saw it would probably go on sale, and waited three whole impatient months. She paid half price and bought a second game with the rest.
explain: Ollie lost his gift card in the worst way possible: he threw the paper with the code in the recycling. He felt sick. His mum helped him contact the shop with the receipt, and luckily they found the card again. Now Ollie knows a code on paper is money on paper. Kai earned his gift card himself, selling old toys at a car boot sale (a market where people sell old things from their cars) on a freezing Sunday, and because he remembered the cold, he spent it far more carefully than any birthday money before.
explain: Theo's grandma sent twenty pounds in cash, and Theo announced it was all going on games. His mum suggested half for games and half for saving; he grumbled like a grumpy pirate, but agreed. Zara got a gift card for a game she does not even play, felt a little disappointed, thanked her uncle anyway, and swapped it with her mum for one she could use. Ruby's friend asked to borrow her gift card, and Ruby said no, kindly — her mum had said the card was for her, and money between friends goes wrong when the rules are fuzzy.
explain: Gift money can make other people happy too. Priya used her aunt's gift card to buy her aunt's favourite character in the game, and her aunt laughed so much that she started playing herself. Noah bought a game for his little sister Isla as well as his own, and Isla followed him round the house for a week saying «thank you» until he begged her to stop. Jayden kept a notebook of everything he bought with his gift cards; at the end of the year he read it and saw that most of the things he had paid for he had never really used. He called the notebook «the book of truth».
fact: A gift card holds real money that can only be spent in one shop or service.
fact: The code on a gift card works like money; if someone else sees it, they can spend it.
fact: Keep gift card codes and receipts safe until the card is used.
fact: Spreading out spending makes the fun last longer than spending everything at once.
fact: Games often go on sale for much less than their first price, so waiting can save money.
fact: Saving a part of every gift adds up faster than most people expect.
fact: Money you earned yourself usually makes you spend more carefully.
fact: Writing down what you spend shows you the truth about where your money goes.
fact: It is fine to say no, kindly, when someone asks to borrow your money.
fact: Spending some of your money on someone else can be one of the best ways to enjoy it.
fact: Saying thank you for a gift matters even when the gift is not quite right.
fact: Anyone who asks you to pay them in gift cards, or to read them a gift card code, is almost certainly a scammer.
exercise: How did Leo feel the morning after spending all three gift cards? | Flat, with nothing left to look forward to | Rich and excited, with plans for the whole week | Proud of how well he had planned it all | Angry at the people who gave him the cards
exercise: What did Leo's gran mean? | Money spent fast is fun for a short time and then gone | Gift cards should always be spent quickly, before they run out | Hot dog emotes are a waste of everybody's time | Birthdays should have no presents
exercise: How did Mia get two games for the price of one? | She waited for a sale and paid half price | She asked the shop nicely for a second game for free | She borrowed money from Theo | She found a secret code online
exercise: Why did Ollie feel sick? | He threw away the paper with the code | His gift card was for the wrong game entirely | He spent the card on the wrong thing | His mum spent his gift card
exercise: What helped Ollie get his gift card back? | The receipt, and contacting the shop with his mum | Shouting at the recycling bin | Asking a stranger online for help | Buying a new card with the same code
exercise: Why did Kai spend his gift card carefully? | He had earned it himself on a freezing Sunday | His mum told him to buy only one thing with it | The card was very small | He did not like any games
exercise: What did Theo's mum suggest for grandma's twenty pounds? | Half for games and half for saving | All of it on games straight away | All of it saved in the bank for ever | All of it given back to grandma
exercise: How much did Theo keep for games? | Ten pounds | Twenty pounds | Five pounds | Fifteen pounds
exercise: Zara got a gift card for a game she does not play. What did she do? | Thanked her uncle and swapped it with her mum | Threw the card away in a huff, then sulked all day | Told her uncle it was a bad present | Sold the code to a stranger online
exercise: Why did Ruby say no to lending her gift card? | It was for her, and money between friends goes wrong when rules are fuzzy | She did not like her friend | Gift cards break if you lend them | She wanted to spend it on her friend
exercise: What happened after Priya bought her aunt's favourite character? | Her aunt laughed and started playing too | Her aunt asked for the money back the next day | Her aunt was cross about the game | Nothing, her aunt never found out
exercise: How did Noah's sister Isla react to her game? | She followed him for a week saying thank you | She asked him to take it back and swap it for another | She did not notice it | She was cross he didn't buy two
exercise: What did Jayden learn from «the book of truth»? | Most things he bought he never really used | He had spent nothing all year | Notebooks are a waste of paper | Gift cards were not worth having
exercise: What is wise to do with a gift card code before you use it? | Keep it and the receipt somewhere safe | Post a photo of it online so friends can see | Read it out to your friends | Write it on your hand
exercise: Which is a kind of courage in this lesson? | Saying no, kindly, when a friend asks to borrow money | Spending all your money to impress friends | Pretending to like a present and then binning it | Lending your card because you are afraid to say no
exercise: A message says «you've won, just send us a gift card code to claim». What is it? | A scam, never send gift card codes | A real prize, send it fast before it goes | A game update you must answer | A message from your bank
test: «got 3 gift cards, spending em ALL tonight lol». Wise reply? | Spread it out so the fun lasts longer | Spend them fast before the codes disappear | Buy three of the same skin | Give them away to strangers
test: Mia: «game's £40 now, might be £20 in the sale». What could waiting get her? | The same game for half the price | A newer version of the game that is worse | Nothing, prices never drop in sales | A fine from the shop for waiting too long
test: Ollie's mum finds the gift card paper in the recycling. Lesson? | A code on paper is money on paper, so keep it safe | Paper codes are worthless | Recycling bins are safe places for codes | The shop always prints a new code
test: «borrow ur gift card? I'll pay u back, promise». Ruby? | A kind no is fine if the rules aren't clear | She must lend it, or she'll lose the friend for good | Give the code and forget it | Lend it only if he begs twice
test: Theo, holding grandma's £20: «it's MINE, all of it on games». Wise plan? | Save some and spend some | Spend it all in one go, tonight | Hide it so mum can't ask about it | Give it all to a friend to look after
test: Zara: «ugh, a gift card for a game I don't even play». Best thing to do first? | Say thank you to her uncle | Tell her uncle it's useless to her | Throw it straight in the bin | Sell it to a stranger online
test: Kai sold his old toys to buy his card. Why will he likely spend it carefully? | He knows how much work the money took | The card is harder to use than other cards | Car boot money is worth more | He is not allowed to spend it
test: Jayden reads his notebook in December. What's it good for? | Seeing the truth about what he spends on | Remembering all his friends' passwords | Proving he's better at games than his mates | Writing down cheat codes
test: a stranger online: «read me ur gift card number, I'll double it». What is this? | A scam to steal the money on the card | A kind offer from a rich player who likes him | A game feature for lucky players | A way to check the card works
test: Noah bought his sister a game too. What did he find out? | Giving can be one of the best ways to enjoy money | Sisters never say thank you | Gifts are a waste of money | He should never share anything
test: Priya: «I'll get my aunt's fave character in the game too». What might happen? | Her aunt might laugh and join in | Her aunt will be cross with her for ever | The game will refund the card to her aunt | Nothing can ever happen from a gift
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a gift card code ought to be kept secure until used. | Any person who obtains the code may spend its value | The code expires upon being read aloud | The code may only be used by a parent | The code becomes valid only after being shown to others
exam: Whereas a sum of twenty pounds is received and one half thereof is reserved for saving, determine the amount remaining for expenditure upon games. | Ten pounds | Twenty pounds | Five pounds | Fifteen pounds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a request, from an unknown party, for payment by means of gift card codes? | It is almost certainly fraudulent and ought to be refused | It is a lawful and customary method of claiming a prize | It is an established practice of banks | It is permissible where the sum is small
exam: Select the response most consistent with established guidance concerning the deferral of a purchase until a reduction in price. | Deferral may permit the same item to be acquired at a lower cost | Deferral invariably increases the eventual cost | Deferral is prohibited in respect of games | Deferral renders the purchase invalid
